WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.872
March 28

2
00:00:02.873 --> 00:00:05.399
Dear Daddy Longlegs,

3
00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.218
So many good things have happened!

4
00:00:09.219 --> 00:00:14.440
I won the short story contest that the college magazine holds every year.

5
00:00:14.441 --> 00:00:16.975
And I'm just a sophomore!

6
00:00:16.976 --> 00:00:21.960
The other contestants were mostly seniors.

7
00:00:21.961 --> 00:00:28.488
When I saw my name posted, I couldn't believe I'd won.

8
00:00:28.489 --> 00:00:32.416
First prize is twenty-five dollars.

9
00:00:32.417 --> 00:00:36.336
I also got a part in the spring play, As You Like It

10
00:00:36.337 --> 00:00:38.609
. That's something else that Shakespeare

11
00:00:38.610 --> 00:00:41.148
wrote. Besides Hamlet ,

12
00:00:41.149 --> 00:00:48.157
of course. Last but not least . . . Julia, Sallie, and I are traveling to New York City

13
00:00:48.158 --> 00:00:51.082
next weekend. We're going to do some

14
00:00:51.083 --> 00:00:55.282
spring shopping. Master Jervie has invited us to the theater

15
00:00:55.283 --> 00:00:58.190
on Saturday. Julia will stay at home with

16
00:00:58.191 --> 00:01:01.714
her family. But Sallie and I are going to stay at

17
00:01:01.715 --> 00:01:05.673
a hotel. I've never been in a hotel or a

18
00:01:05.674 --> 00:01:08.806
theater! And what do you think we're going to see at the

19
00:01:08.807 --> 00:01:12.783
theater? Hamlet .

20
00:01:12.784 --> 00:01:16.916
Imagine that! We studied it for a month in

21
00:01:16.917 --> 00:01:19.686
Shakespeare class. I know it

22
00:01:19.687 --> 00:01:23.436
by heart. I'm so excited that I can

23
00:01:23.437 --> 00:01:24.225
hardly

24
00:01:24.226 --> 00:01:26.570
sleep.

25
00:01:26.571 --> 00:01:28.101
Yours, Judy

26
00:01:28.102 --> 00:01:30.309
April 7 Dear

27
00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:35.743
Daddy Longlegs, My goodness! New York City

28
00:01:35.744 --> 00:01:40.818
is big! Do you really live in all

29
00:01:40.819 --> 00:01:45.259
that confusion? It'll take me months to recover from just two

30
00:01:45.260 --> 00:01:48.608
days there. I can't begin to tell you everything

31
00:01:48.609 --> 00:01:52.393
I saw. But I guess you know what New York

32
00:01:52.394 --> 00:01:57.219
is like. Sallie, Julia, and I went shopping on

33
00:01:57.220 --> 00:02:02.606
Saturday morning. Those shop windows were filled with so many

34
00:02:02.607 --> 00:02:06.255
lovely things. They make you want to devote your life

35
00:02:06.256 --> 00:02:10.413
to clothes. "I need some new hats,"

36
00:02:10.414 --> 00:02:15.116
Julia said. We entered the most gorgeous shop on

37
00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:18.401
the block. The walls were white

38
00:02:18.402 --> 00:02:23.445
and gold. There were blue silk curtains and

39
00:02:23.446 --> 00:02:27.556
gold chairs. A pretty blond woman in a black gown

40
00:02:27.557 --> 00:02:31.463
welcomed us. It was like paying a social call at

41
00:02:31.464 --> 00:02:36.379
someone's home! "Please take a seat in front of the mirror," she said

42
00:02:36.380 --> 00:02:40.657
to Julia. Then the woman brought over a selection of

43
00:02:40.658 --> 00:02:48.741
beautiful hats. "Each one is lovelier than the last,"

44
00:02:48.742 --> 00:02:55.301
Sallie said. I could only nod and watch as Julia tried

45
00:02:55.302 --> 00:03:00.553
them on. At last Julia pointed to the two loveliest hats

46
00:03:00.554 --> 00:03:03.212
of all. "I'll take both

47
00:03:03.213 --> 00:03:06.702
of those." She handed the woman a card with her name

48
00:03:06.703 --> 00:03:09.781
and address. "Please send them to

49
00:03:09.782 --> 00:03:15.194
this address." "Julia never asked the prices!" Sallie whispered

50
00:03:15.195 --> 00:03:18.098
to me. "I know!" I

51
00:03:18.099 --> 00:03:23.546
whispered back. "I cannot imagine being able to buy any hat I

52
00:03:23.547 --> 00:03:27.697
wanted!" Next we met Master Jervie for lunch at

53
00:03:27.698 --> 00:03:30.208
Sherry's. Have you ever been

54
00:03:30.209 --> 00:03:34.268
there? Picture Sherry's—it's a really fancy

55
00:03:34.269 --> 00:03:40.237
restaurant. Then picture the dining room at the orphanage with its plain white dishes and worn-out

56
00:03:40.238 --> 00:03:42.919
tables. Quite the

57
00:03:42.920 --> 00:03:46.009
contrast! I ate my fish with the wrong

58
00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:50.292
fork. But the waiter very kindly gave me another

59
00:03:50.293 --> 00:03:53.822
one. I don't think anyone

60
00:03:53.823 --> 00:03:58.240
noticed. After lunch we went straight to the

61
00:03:58.241 --> 00:04:01.226
theater. I sat between Sallie and Master

62
00:04:01.227 --> 00:04:04.315
Jervie. "Are you eager to see the play?" he asked

63
00:04:04.316 --> 00:04:08.264
me. "Oh yes! I've been looking forward to

64
00:04:08.265 --> 00:04:13.369
it!" I was so excited, and the play hadn't even started

65
00:04:13.370 --> 00:04:18.941
yet. Hamlet was amazing! Dazzling!

66
00:04:18.942 --> 00:04:23.448
Marvelous! "Isn't Shakespeare wonderful?" Master Jervie said to me at

67
00:04:23.449 --> 00:04:28.035
intermission. "I thought I liked Hamlet while I was reading it, but now . .

68
00:04:28.036 --> 00:04:32.574
. It's so much better onstage than reading it at

69
00:04:32.575 --> 00:04:34.893
school." Master Jervie

70
00:04:34.894 --> 00:04:37.525
smiled. I'm sure my eyes were wide as

71
00:04:37.526 --> 00:04:39.238
saucers. But I don't

72
00:04:39.239 --> 00:04:42.331
care. Maybe I'll become an actress instead of a

73
00:04:42.332 --> 00:04:45.928
writer. I'll send you tickets for all my

74
00:04:45.929 --> 00:04:49.226
shows. Then I'll smile at you from the

75
00:04:49.227 --> 00:04:52.258
stage. Please wear a red rose in your

76
00:04:52.259 --> 00:04:55.876
buttonhole. I'd hate to smile at the wrong

77
00:04:55.877 --> 00:04:58.830
man. We returned to school on Saturday

78
00:04:58.831 --> 00:05:05.743
night. We ate supper on the train. There were little tables with pink

79
00:05:05.744 --> 00:05:11.741
lamps. "I never heard of meals being served on a train," I said as we sat

80
00:05:11.742 --> 00:05:16.678
down. "Where on earth did you grow up?" Julia

81
00:05:16.679 --> 00:05:19.332
asked. "In a village," I said

82
00:05:19.333 --> 00:05:23.074
quietly. I hoped she wouldn't ask any more questions.

83
00:05:23.075 --> 00:05:26.466
"Did you ever travel?" she asked.

84
00:05:26.467 --> 00:05:29.164
"Not till I went to college.

85
00:05:29.165 --> 00:05:35.142
And then it was only 160 miles, and we didn't eat in the dining car."

86
00:05:35.143 --> 00:05:39.372
She smiled. "I can't imagine that.

87
00:05:39.373 --> 00:05:43.218
You say the funniest things!"

88
00:05:43.219 --> 00:05:48.298
I try not to say funny things, but they pop out when I'm surprised.

89
00:05:48.299 --> 00:05:52.459
At least I don't make as many mistakes as I used to.

90
00:05:52.460 --> 00:05:56.879
I'm learning how people who didn't grow up in orphanages live.

91
00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.096
So I feel more comfortable around the other girls now.

92
00:06:01.097 --> 00:06:05.455
I forgot to tell you about our flowers!

93
00:06:05.456 --> 00:06:10.888
Master Jervie gave us each a bunch of violets and lilies of the valley.

94
00:06:10.889 --> 00:06:12.806
Wasn't that sweet of him?

95
00:06:12.807 --> 00:06:16.050
I never used to care much for men.

96
00:06:16.051 --> 00:06:20.144
But I'm changing my mind.

97
00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:21.491
Yours always,

98
00:06:21.492 --> 00:06:25.234
Judy

